1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,732
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,732 --> 00:00:06,531
This is chapter twenty-five: shared state.

3
00:00:06,981 --> 00:00:12,006
The moment two threads touch the same data, three different things can go wrong.

4
00:00:12,006 --> 00:00:17,157
Telling them apart is the key to fixing them, because each needs a different tool.

5
00:00:18,057 --> 00:00:20,056
Here's an inventory counter.

6
00:00:20,056 --> 00:00:25,124
Reserving stock is one line: available equals available minus quantity.

7
00:00:25,124 --> 00:00:32,691
Run it from two threads, ten thousand reservations each, and the result is wrong, and different every run.

8
00:00:33,141 --> 00:00:37,382
That one line is really three steps: read, subtract, write.

9
00:00:37,382 --> 00:00:43,419
Two threads can both read the same value before either writes, so an update is lost.

10
00:00:43,419 --> 00:00:49,888
In the inventory engine, that means overselling: two customers both reserve the last unit.

11
00:00:50,788 --> 00:00:53,271
There are three separate problems.

12
00:00:53,271 --> 00:00:58,236
Atomicity: a compound action gets interleaved, like the lost update.

13
00:00:58,236 --> 00:01:01,668
Fixed by synchronized, locks or atomic classes.

14
00:01:02,118 --> 00:01:06,028
Visibility: one thread's write is never seen by another.

15
00:01:06,028 --> 00:01:10,498
A loop keeps running after another thread sets running to false.

16
00:01:10,498 --> 00:01:13,361
Fixed by volatile, synchronized or final.

17
00:01:13,811 --> 00:01:20,876
Ordering: the compiler or CPU reorders operations, so another thread sees a half-built object.

18
00:01:20,876 --> 00:01:23,432
Fixed by volatile or synchronized.

19
00:01:23,882 --> 00:01:29,246
The key point: volatile fixes visibility and ordering, but not atomicity.

20
00:01:29,246 --> 00:01:33,802
Most misuse of volatile comes from not separating these three.

21
00:01:34,702 --> 00:01:37,797
Visibility sounds theoretical, but it isn't.

22
00:01:37,797 --> 00:01:42,510
Cores have their own caches, and the JIT keeps values in registers.

23
00:01:42,510 --> 00:01:51,655
Here, the JIT sees that nothing in this thread writes running, so it's allowed to read it once and compile the loop to while true.

24
00:01:51,655 --> 00:01:53,625
The stop call is never seen.

25
00:01:54,075 --> 00:01:56,497
Marking the flag volatile fixes it.

26
00:01:56,497 --> 00:02:04,869
This is the canonical correct use of volatile: one writer, many readers, and the new value doesn't depend on the old one.

27
00:02:05,319 --> 00:02:14,877
And because the hoisting is a legal optimisation, the bug usually disappears under a debugger or the interpreter, and appears only in production.

28
00:02:15,777 --> 00:02:16,776
Here's the fix.

29
00:02:16,776 --> 00:02:23,572
A synchronized block makes the check and the update one atomic step, so the result is right every run.

30
00:02:23,572 --> 00:02:33,633
synchronized is reentrant, and it creates a happens-before edge: everything done before releasing the lock is visible to the next thread that takes it.

31
00:02:34,083 --> 00:02:36,613
Notice the read is synchronized too.

32
00:02:36,613 --> 00:02:40,057
Without it, available could return a stale value.

33
00:02:40,057 --> 00:02:44,344
Synchronizing only writes is a half-fix that appears to work.

34
00:02:44,794 --> 00:02:47,661
And the lock is a private object, not this.

35
00:02:47,661 --> 00:02:56,463
Locking on this publishes your lock, so any caller can synchronize on your object and stall your internals, or deadlock against you.

36
00:02:57,363 --> 00:02:59,856
volatile doesn't make plus plus safe.

37
00:02:59,856 --> 00:03:05,716
It makes each read and each write visible, but does nothing about the gap between them.

38
00:03:05,716 --> 00:03:11,038
Use an AtomicInteger, or a LongAdder when many threads hammer the same counter.

39
00:03:11,488 --> 00:03:16,474
The same race hides in check-then-act code, with no arithmetic at all.

40
00:03:16,474 --> 00:03:20,605
Two threads can both see the key is missing, and both put.

41
00:03:20,605 --> 00:03:25,591
Any test followed by an act on shared state must be atomic as a whole.

42
00:03:25,591 --> 00:03:28,939
Use one operation, like putIfAbsent or compute.

43
00:03:29,839 --> 00:03:33,050
wait and notify build producer-consumer buffers.

44
00:03:33,050 --> 00:03:35,792
wait releases the monitor while it waits.

45
00:03:35,792 --> 00:03:38,133
The whole lesson is the while loop.

46
00:03:38,133 --> 00:03:47,499
On waking, a thread must re-check its condition, because the spec allows spurious wakeups, and another thread may have taken the item first.

47
00:03:47,499 --> 00:03:51,044
An if here gives you an exception about once a month.

48
00:03:51,494 --> 00:03:55,800
You must hold the monitor to call these, and prefer notifyAll.

49
00:03:55,800 --> 00:04:03,230
But in practice, almost every correct use of wait and notify is reimplementing a blocking queue, less well.

50
00:04:03,230 --> 00:04:04,827
Use ArrayBlockingQueue.

51
00:04:05,727 --> 00:04:08,233
Here's a deadlock you can reproduce.

52
00:04:08,233 --> 00:04:10,391
A transfer locks from, then to.

53
00:04:10,391 --> 00:04:15,193
Thread A transfers milk to rice, and thread B transfers rice to milk.

54
00:04:15,193 --> 00:04:21,040
A holds milk and wants rice, B holds rice and wants milk, and neither ever proceeds.

55
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:23,198
The fix is a global lock order.

56
00:04:23,198 --> 00:04:29,045
Sort the two items, here by SKU code, and every thread locks them in the same order.

57
00:04:29,495 --> 00:04:34,467
A deadlock needs four conditions at once, and breaking any one prevents it.

58
00:04:34,467 --> 00:04:42,755
Lock ordering is the practical answer, because it's something you can see in code review rather than have to catch in a test.

59
00:04:42,755 --> 00:04:47,927
And when one happens, jcmd Thread dot print reports monitor deadlocks by name.

60
00:04:48,827 --> 00:04:50,131
What does it cost?

61
00:04:50,131 --> 00:04:56,217
An uncontended synchronized block is a single compare-and-swap on the object header.

62
00:04:56,217 --> 00:04:57,086
Nanoseconds.

63
00:04:57,086 --> 00:04:59,984
Synchronized is slow is outdated advice.

64
00:04:59,984 --> 00:05:04,187
It's contention that's expensive, and no lock avoids that.

65
00:05:04,637 --> 00:05:10,789
A volatile read is essentially free on x86, and a write costs about a compare-and-swap.

66
00:05:10,789 --> 00:05:18,567
The barrier also stops reordering, which is what makes double-checked locking correct, but only with volatile.

67
00:05:19,017 --> 00:05:20,162
And a subtle one.

68
00:05:20,162 --> 00:05:29,586
The spec lets a non-volatile long or double write happen as two thirty-two-bit halves, so a reader could see a value that was never written.

69
00:05:29,586 --> 00:05:31,135
Volatile prevents that.

70
00:05:32,035 --> 00:05:33,226
Production rules.

71
00:05:33,226 --> 00:05:40,656
Best of all, avoid shared mutable state: immutable objects, confinement to one thread, or message passing.

72
00:05:40,656 --> 00:05:44,161
Every lock you don't need is a bug you can't have.

73
00:05:44,161 --> 00:05:46,334
Lock on a private final object.

74
00:05:46,334 --> 00:05:47,876
Synchronize reads too.

75
00:05:47,876 --> 00:05:51,311
Keep critical sections short, with no I/O inside.

76
00:05:51,311 --> 00:05:53,203
Impose a global lock order.

77
00:05:53,203 --> 00:05:56,077
And comment which lock guards each field.

78
00:05:56,527 --> 00:05:59,629
Never assume volatile makes plus plus safe.

79
00:05:59,629 --> 00:06:01,360
Never wait inside an if.

80
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:06,625
Never lock on string literals or boxed values, which are shared JVM-wide.

81
00:06:06,625 --> 00:06:09,510
Never hold a lock across a network call.

82
00:06:09,510 --> 00:06:12,755
Never let lock order depend on the arguments.

83
00:06:12,755 --> 00:06:15,352
And never use sleep to avoid a race.

84
00:06:15,352 --> 00:06:18,309
It just hides the bug until load changes.

85
00:06:19,209 --> 00:06:19,843
To recap.

86
00:06:19,843 --> 00:06:24,638
Shared state has three problems: atomicity, visibility and ordering.

87
00:06:24,638 --> 00:06:30,279
volatile solves visibility, not atomicity, so use atomics for read-modify-write.

88
00:06:30,279 --> 00:06:33,171
Synchronize reads too, on a private lock.

89
00:06:33,171 --> 00:06:36,978
Wait in a while loop, or better, use a blocking queue.

90
00:06:36,978 --> 00:06:40,222
And prevent deadlock with a global lock order.

91
00:06:40,672 --> 00:06:46,791
Next is chapter twenty-six, the Java memory model, which explains why all of this works.

92
00:06:46,791 --> 00:06:48,181
Thanks for watching.
